"Design details and open problems (2026-08-12). WHY C+D BEATS A AND B AT THE CORE: both rejected designs make availability a RUNTIME COMPUTATION over stat blocks (ranking in A, condition evaluation in B), which is exactly what makes the result hard for a player to predict; C moves the computation to preparation time where the player watches it happen, so the loadout screen can show the literal six actions each swap will yield -- prediction becomes reading. A's spear-technique-while-unarmed nonsense and B's wrong-stance confusion both vanish because invalid combinations are unrepresentable after assignment-time validation. FIT WITH EXISTING MACHINERY: armament/stance stat contributions ride the existing stat-block cache pipeline (equipment_stat_block_cache is already declared and unused); the active battle-action set becomes a derived component recomputed by a system watching loadout mutations via the new auto-derived dirty flags; the pinned-actions bar naturally becomes the active loadout's actions plus swap-loadout buttons. OPEN PROBLEMS: (a) DISARM/LOSS mid-fight invalidates the active loadout -- proposal: invalidation forces a transition to a forced stance ('improvised'), making the fallback an explicit modeled state rather than a silent degradation, consistent with forced-stance machinery; (b) how many prepared loadouts may be carried (swap targets) and whether swapping armaments only (same stance) is cheaper than a full swap; (c) default technique assignments for forced stances must ship in assets so prone is never empty (authored default, replaceable -- not a runtime fallback); (d) do enemies use the same loadout machinery (probably yes for fairness/simulation-purity, with authored fixed loadouts in blobs); (e) whether the six is exactly six (stance slot count as stance property, most stances six). VALUE-RANKING (A) noted for possible reuse: AI enemy action selection and suggested default slot assignments."